<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Emboabas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1709">1709</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Emboabas was a conflict in the gold fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, between the Paulistas who had discovered the gold and the newcomers from Portugal and other parts of Brazil, called emboabas. The newcomers, led by Manuel Nunes Viana, drove the Paulistas from the main mining districts. The crown responded by creating the Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> de Ouro in 1709.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-emboabas.jpg" alt="War of the Emboabas" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerra_dos_Emboabas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the 1690s alluvial gold was being taken from the streams of the region then called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> dos Matos Gerais, and within two decades tens of thousands of people came from Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, Pernambuco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, with large numbers of enslaved Indigenous and African workers. The paulista bandeirantes claimed the mines as their discovery and in 1700 the council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> asked for exclusive control, which was refused. The word emboaba was a pejorative of the paulista língua geral meaning outsider or invader; its derivation is disputed. Quarrels over claims, water and supply contracts multiplied from 1706, and in 1707 two leading paulistas were lynched at the Arraial Novo do Rio das Mortes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting spread through the districts of Sabará, Cachoeira do Campo and the Rio das Mortes, where the paulistas looked to the former bandeirante Manuel de Borba Gato. Its best-known episode is the Capão da Traição, the Thicket of Treason: according to traditional accounts, paulistas besieged in a wood by emboaba forces under Bento do Amaral Coutinho surrendered on a promise of safety and many were then killed, though modern historians question the numbers. The emboabas proclaimed Manuel Nunes Viana governor of the mines, until Antônio de Albuquerque Coelho de Carvalho, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, arrived early in 1709, deposed him and restored royal control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 3 November 1709 the crown broke up the old Captaincy of São Vicente, creating the Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> de Ouro and a separate Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, and São Paulo became a city and the new capital. The mining towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica</a> and Sabará followed in 1711 and São João del-Rei in 1713. Many paulistas moved west into what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> and Mato Grosso, while emboaba merchants and office-holders formed new elites tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. Tighter royal control helped gold output rise in the first half of the 18th century. The conflict is the subject of Diná Silveira de Queirós's novel A Muralha of 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Emboabas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>the War of the Emboabas</b> between Paulista pioneers and newcomers in the gold fields, the crown bought out the old Captaincy of São Vicente and created the Captaincy of São Paulo and Minas de Ouro in 1709, with São Paulo as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-paulo">Captaincy of São Paulo</a></li></ul></section>
